body:
## Problem

A fixed-frame reference can intentionally contain overlapping axis text. Cartesian labels already accept `overlap: 'allow'` for label-label collisions, but an axis title always rejects label-title collisions. Faithful static reproduction currently needs unsupported title ownership or altered source text/geometry.

Reference specimen0107 has 150 tick rectangles in a 300×81 plot /668×160 frame. All150 paints match a public Rug+static-highlight program. Its original Y title bbox is `[147.5,33.015625,160.5,106.984375]`, intersecting three original label bboxes. With `labels.overlap:'allow'`, the installed candidate953679ba rejects `createYAxisTitle`: `The y-axis title overlaps the axis labels.` This is an opt-in capability gap, not a request to weaken default layout checks.

## Reproduction

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';

chart()
  .createCanvas({
    width: 500, height: 220,
    margin: { left: 320, right: 20, top: 20, bottom: 60 }
  })
  .createData({ values: Array.from({ length: 10 }, (_, i) => ({
    rank: i + 1, group: 'An intentionally long category ' + (i + 1)
  })) })
  .createScatterPlot({
    x: 'rank', y: { field: 'group', fieldType: 'nominal' }, guides: false
  })
  .createAxes({
    x: false,
    y: {
      ticksAndLabels: { labels: { fontSize: 14, overlap: 'allow' } },
      title: { text: 'Group', fontSize: 11, offset: 80 }
    }
  });
```

Reproduced on the immutable local953679ba package. No reference image pixels were inspected; original SVG text geometry and native error diagnoses were checked. No model request was used.

## Proposed existing-API extension

Add `overlap?: 'error' | 'allow'` to Cartesian `AxisTitleOptions`, including complete axes and owned title editors. Omission remains strict. `'allow'` suppresses only label-title collision rejection for that title; label-label overlap and Canvas bounds remain separate checks. Apply the same owner policy when labels are created/edited after a title. Keep named-axis, facet, rematerialization, persistence and Basic/Full behavior consistent. Reject invalid values atomically, and allow an explicit reset to `'error'` only when the resulting layout is valid.

No new action, renderer primitive, global geometric epsilon, font shrinking, data change or default output change. Title Canvas overflow stays an error.

## Verification and release

- Primitive/public exact same-run decoded output for the approved overlap target.
- Default strict output/error behavior, both edit orders, reset/rollback, named owners, facets, scale/Canvas/data rematerialization, persistence validation, public types and installed package consumers.
- Synchronized current contract, canonical action/type references, search and generated docs.
- Full current chart-cohort native compatibility before local adoption.

Standing user authorization explicitly approves all in-scope future design questions. Preserve the 200-success-family public release cadence: public1213; next regular checkpoint1413. Keep this issue open until actual public package/docs/dataset verification. Specimen0107 stays unadmitted with zero model requests until its full native/source qualification succeeds.

## Problem

Basic field encoding reassignment calls `program.editScale`, which is intentionally absent from the Basic public API. An existing ordinal dash mapping cannot replace its scale range even though the field encoding action is available.

Confirmed before the categorical dash fallback change, using the installed unreleased `49adee7d23e3651000ccb425fbf2995d5a163348` candidate (package manifest `0.0.21`); reproduced again in the working source. This is a pre-existing gap, separate from #241. Registry-package behavior has not been claimed or verified here.

## Public reproduction

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction/basic';

const program = chart()
  .createCanvas()
  .createData({values: [
    {x: 0, y: 1, group: 'a'},
    {x: 1, y: 2, group: 'b'}
  ]})
  .createLinePlot({x: 'x', y: 'y', guides: false})
  .encodeStrokeDash({field: 'group'});

program.encodeStrokeDash({
  field: 'group',
  scale: {range: ['dotted']}
});
```

Actual: `TypeError: program.editScale is not a function` from the shared encoding-scale application path.

Expected: supported Basic encoding reassignment should update and rematerialize the bound scale through an internally available wrapped action. Preserve the Basic public method boundary, immutable input/trace and shared-consumer atomicity; do not add the Full public editor family to Basic as a workaround. Audit other field-encoding channels using the same path.

## Tracking and delivery

Recorded while validating #241. Its installed-package tests separately cover Basic first construction and Full editing/reassignment; this issue is not represented as fixed. Keep the current chart translation sequential and preserve existing completed chart epochs. Implement and verify as a separate coherent change; public integration remains the next 200-family checkpoint at 1,413 successful families.

## Problem

An omitted right-side legend offset ignores an existing right Y axis. A chart with ample right margin is rejected when `createLegend({})` puts the legend into that axis lane. The symmetric left-side case needs the same default placement rule.

Reproduced with the published `ggaction@0.0.21` artifact at `e93500081b6dde6be7614e3e9352e947bec5908d`:

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';

const p = chart()
  .createCanvas({
    plot: { width: 300, height: 200 },
    margin: { left: 40, right: 220, top: 40, bottom: 40 }
  })
  .createData({ values: [
    { x: 1, y: 1, category: 'A' },
    { x: 2, y: 2, category: 'B' }
  ] })
  .createScatterPlot({ x: 'x', y: 'y', guides: false })
  .encodeColor({ field: 'category' })
  .createAxes({
    x: false,
    y: [{ id: 'rightAxis', scale: 'y', position: 'right' }]
  });

p.createLegend({});
// The right rightAxis-axis labels and color legend overlap;
// guides require more margin space or different offsets.

p.createLegend({ offset: 90 }); // Builds with the same Canvas/data/axes.
```

A complete source chart with 471 original rows, two Y scales and a 576×364 Canvas reproduces this before any model request. Its raw nested metadata can already be stored by the library; this failure is separate from the translation input validator.

## Expected behavior / proposed scope

Extend the existing inferred top/bottom axis-clearance policy to omitted external left/right offsets. Use actual same-edge axis line/ticks/labels/title occupied bounds, preserve the family default gap and the plot-top vertical anchor, and retain the stored offset plus existing `inferredOffset` intent.

Explicit offsets, free positions, in-plot corners and legacy layouts keep their authored anchors and normal collision checks. Do not expand the Canvas, change data/scales, hide labels, or introduce a new public action/parameter. Preserve order-independent replay for axis edits/removal, Canvas changes, legend groups and persistence; outputs without a relevant side axis must remain unchanged.

## Validation

Cover the minimal right-axis case and its left mirror; occupied borders/strokes; categorical, continuous, size and multi-block legends; explicit-offset rejection/immutability; axis-before-legend versus legend-before-axis; axis editing/removal and persistence; Full/Basic installed consumers; and existing default chart output compatibility.

The user's standing in-scope design approval applies. Implementation will stay in the tested batch candidate and will not be published before the next 200-family release checkpoint.

## Problem

`encodeStrokeDash` accepts only `fieldType: "nominal"`. An ordered categorical field cannot retain its `"ordinal"` meaning, even with a complete explicit domain and valid dash range. The error currently refers to a color field:

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const program = chart()
  .createCanvas({ width: 260, height: 180, margin: 24 })
  .createData({ values: [
    { level: 2, x: 0, y: 20 }, { level: 2, x: 1, y: 20 },
    { level: 1, x: 0, y: 10 }, { level: 1, x: 1, y: 10 }
  ] })
  .createLinePlot({ x: "x", y: "y", guides: false });

program.encodeStrokeDash({
  field: "level",
  fieldType: "ordinal",
  scale: { domain: [1, 2], range: [[1, 0], [4, 2]] }
});
// Error: Unsupported color field type "ordinal".
```

This blocks an ordered-category comparison during chart translation. No paid generation was dispatched for that source, and the previous immutable program remains unchanged after rejection. The current public declaration is nominal-only as well; this is a missing categorical capability rather than a documentation retrieval failure.

## Proposed existing-API extension

- Accept `"nominal" | "ordinal"` in the existing field mode; keep nominal as the omitted default.
- Preserve the exact type in semantic state. Reuse the existing categorical validator and ordinal dash mapping.
- Keep automatic domain order at first appearance; explicit domain order wins. Do not sort rows or silently coerce category values.
- Use the same behavior through line/rule, existing line facade and bulk channel assignment.
- Preserve path identity, one raw appearance value per series, missing-value policy, scale edits, legends, selections and atomic rejection.
- Keep constant-mode restrictions and direct/named pattern validation, including valid zero-gap patterns, unchanged.

## Verification

Cover semantic intent, explicit/automatic order, early/late assignment, line/rule/facade/bulk paths, scale and constant reassignment, legend/selection rematerialization, persistence and render exports. Matching explicit nominal/ordinal domain/range must produce the same SVG and decoded pixels. Invalid types and conflicting per-series values must remain immutable failures. Synchronize types, current contract, generated action cards/references and public docs.

The user has already approved in-scope future API designs. Implementation will be collected in the tested candidate branch and integrated with the next regular 200-completed-chart release; this issue remains open until integration.

## Problem

In published ggaction 0.0.21, shared facet legend inference combines continuous color and continuous stroke as an ordinal legend. Both individual continuous encodings and their explicit gradient owners already work. No HCL interpolation is involved in this reproduction.

```javascript
import { chart } from "ggaction";
const scale = { domain: [0, 1], range: ["red", "blue"], interpolate: "lab", whitePoint: "d50" };
const base = chart()
  .createCanvas({ width: 800, height: 800, margin: 200 })
  .createData({ values: [
    { x: 0, y: 0, v: 0, g: "a" },
    { x: 1, y: 1, v: 1, g: "b" }
  ] })
  .createScatterPlot({ x: "x", y: "y", guides: false })
  .encodeColor({ field: "v", fieldType: "quantitative", scale })
  .encodeStroke({ field: "v", fieldType: "quantitative", scale });

base.facet({ field: "g", guides: { legend: "shared" } });
// Error: Legend requires resolved ordinal scale "color".

// Existing supported flow succeeds with unchanged data:
base.createLegend({ title: false })
  .facet({ field: "g", guides: { legend: "shared" } });
```

## Expected behavior

Shared legend inference should preserve the two continuous scale owners and produce their continuous legends, consistent with explicit `createLegend` before faceting. It must not reinterpret the quantitative fields as categorical or change the original rows, scale domains, interpolation or paint values. Preserve existing ordinal color/stroke combination behavior.

## Diagnosis and scope

The failing route passes through `prepareAutoLegendSource` / `prepareSharedFacetLegend` into the categorical legend creation plan. The same program with explicit continuous owners succeeds. The earlier immutable program and both original rows remain unchanged after failure.

Reproduced independently against the installed, byte-verified published 0.0.21 package. Found while checking neutral HCL consumers for #227, then reproduced with Lab to establish that this is an independent pre-existing defect. An existing explicit-owner flow avoids the error, so it does not block unrelated chart translation or the neutral-hue fix. No image pixels inspected and no new model request was required.

## Problem

In ggaction 0.0.20, `createIntervalData` and interval-backed chart facades round every bootstrap result to 12 decimal places. A finite positive percentile interval can collapse to zero width around an ordinary-sized center. `createSummaryData` preserves the same sampler's full JavaScript Number values.

## Public reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";
const values = [1, 1.0000000000001, 1.0000000000002,
  1.0000000000003, 1.0000000000004].map(value => ({ group: "a", value }));
const method = { type: "bootstrap", seed: 1 };
const base = chart().createData({ values });
const summary = base.createSummaryData({
  id: "summary", groupBy: "group", aggregates: [
    { field: "value", op: "mean", as: "mean" },
    { field: "value", op: { op: "ciLower", method }, as: "lower" },
    { field: "value", op: { op: "ciUpper", method }, as: "upper" }
  ]
}).semanticSpec.datasets.at(-1).values;
const interval = base.createIntervalData({
  id: "interval", field: "value", groupBy: "group", method,
  as: { center: "mean", lower: "lower", upper: "upper" }
}).semanticSpec.datasets.at(-1).values;
```

Summary: mean `1.0000000000002`, lower `1.00000000000008`, upper `1.0000000000003397`. Interval: mean/lower/upper all `1`. All observations, group order, seed, default 1000 samples and default 0.95 level are identical.

## Cause and correction

`src/grammar/interval.js` applies `stableDecimal` to center and both endpoints after the shared bootstrap kernel. Preserve full finite JavaScript Number results for the explicit bootstrap method, consistently with the approved exact resampling/R7 arithmetic. Keep the existing output policy for normal/Student-t, standard deviation/error and median/IQR unchanged. No new API option, persisted transform shape, random generator, draw bound or renderer is needed.

Verify independent exact small-span and decimal expectations, positive width, scale/translation behavior where representable, immutable input/earlier programs, interval creation/edit/revise/persistence, and interval-backed facade output. Add legacy-output regression coverage and update the current contract/public documentation.

Original exact-bootstrap design approval: #219 and `agent_docs/impl/bootstrap-mean-ci/approval-evidence.json`. This issue records a numerical bug in that approved implementation. No chart pixels were inspected.

## Observed behavior

In `ggaction@0.0.19`, HCL interpolation toward an equal-channel neutral gray rotates toward an arbitrary hue introduced by the RGB→Lab matrix residual. A blue→near-white ramp consequently becomes purple near its middle. Setting the newly supported D50 convention does not resolve this achromatic endpoint case.

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';
const p = chart().createCanvas({width:300,height:180})
  .createData({values:[{x:0,y:0,v:0.5}]})
  .createScatterPlot({x:'x',y:'y',guides:false})
  .encodeColor({field:'v',fieldType:'quantitative',scale:{
    domain:[0,1], range:['#4682b4','#fdfdfd'],
    interpolate:'hcl', whitePoint:'d50'
  }});
```

For a neutral endpoint the hue is undefined; retaining the chromatic endpoint's hue makes this segment equivalent to a straight Lab segment. Current `rgbToHcl` always assigns a finite `atan2` hue, and `mix` always interpolates it, even for equal-channel neutral RGB inputs. Both D50 and D65 controls show an unwanted hue excursion.

## Translation evidence and existing alternative

The original 36-cell heatmap `vl_1314` has a blue→neutral→tan piecewise scale with domain `[-9,0,9]`. Current native HCL/D50 differs by up to **30 RGB channel units** on its actual cells. Using the existing Lab/D50 interpolation, the equivalent two-endpoint domain `[-9,9]`, and the same three uniformly placed RGB stops gives **0 RGB difference on all 36 original cells**. An additional **16,385 evenly spaced samples across the entire domain** also have 0 rounded RGB difference against the original hue-preserving reference. No observations or source values were changed and no tolerance was widened.

The existing equivalent representation means this source can continue translating. This report makes no library implementation or persisted schema change. Please define and test neutral endpoint hue handling (including neutral↔neutral and long-hue behavior) without changing chromatic endpoint behavior or the existing 8-bit rounding policy. HSL/cubehelix achromatic endpoints may warrant a separate audit rather than silently broadening this fix.

## Problem

In published `ggaction@0.0.19` (`f6b50bf5`), the public range encoding type accepts `lower`/`upper` as `string | { datum: number }`, but constant endpoints fail for a rect. The equivalent public `encodeY` + `encodeY2` operations work and preserve the full-height interval.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';
const base = () => chart()
  .createCanvas({width:240,height:160,margin:{left:20,right:20,top:20,bottom:20}})
  .createData({values:[{x0:0,x1:2}]})
  .createRectMark({id:'period'})
  .encodeXRange({lower:'x0',upper:'x1',scale:{domain:[0,2],nice:false}});

base().encodeYRange({
  lower:{datum:45000}, upper:{datum:0},
  scale:{domain:[0,45000],nice:false}
});
// TypeError: encodeY field must be a non-empty string.

// Existing public workaround: y=20 and height=120, as expected.
base()
  .encodeY({datum:45000,scale:{domain:[0,45000],nice:false}})
  .encodeY2({datum:0});
```

## Evidence and scope

`src/actions/encodings/ranged.js` initializes endpoints as `{field:args.lower}` / `{field:args.upper}` and prepares datum endpoints only in the area branch. The broad public endpoint union is therefore misleading for other supported ranged mark kinds. Rect direct datum encodings already work; this is not a request to invent observations or add fields for geometry constants.

Found while preserving the two full-height temporal event rectangles in source `vl_0021` (38 population rows and a separate original 2-row event table). The existing two-encoder workaround reproduces the source geometry, so translation can continue. No library implementation or persisted schema change has been made for this report.

Please align the documented/public type scope with supported mark kinds, or make the range wrapper delegate constant endpoints consistently to the existing encoders, with regression tests for unchanged field endpoints and area behavior.

## Problem

`facet` can partition by an original input field and replay a later `fold`, but cannot partition by the new key produced by that fold. An explicit `data` reference does not help. This prevents a compact program from preserving the original wide input, expressing its reshape through the public data action, and faceting the resulting long table.

Reproduced against published **ggaction 0.0.19**, release commit `f6b50bf55a9de1857f106c7aa519326ce7a86d76`.

## Minimal public reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const p = chart()
  .createCanvas({
    width: 160,
    height: 320,
    margin: { left: 45, right: 10, top: 70, bottom: 50 }
  })
  .createData({ values: [
    { country: "USA", gold: 10, silver: 20 },
    { country: "Canada", gold: 7, silver: 26 }
  ] })
  .createFoldData({
    id: "long", fields: ["gold", "silver"],
    as: { key: "k", value: "v" }
  })
  .createBarPlot({ x: "country", y: "v", guides: false });

p.facet({ field: "k", columns: 2 });
// Facet field "k" is missing from row 0 of partition dataset "data".

p.facet({ field: "k", data: "long", columns: 2 });
// Facet partition dataset "long" is not a common row-preserving
// ancestor of every layer.

// Positive control: supported original-field partition, then local fold replay.
p.facet({ field: "country", columns: 2 });
```

The top margin intentionally provides adequate header space, so both reported failures concern dependency/partition planning rather than layout. The original program remains unchanged after both rejections. Its original input still has two rows and the public fold dataset has four rows.

## Expected capability

An explicit materialized derived table should be usable as a reviewed partition boundary when every visible layer depends on it. For this example the panels must be:

- `gold`: USA 10, Canada 7.
- `silver`: USA 20, Canada 26.

Preserve the original two-row input, declared fold definition, row multiplicity/order, and immutable parent/child lineage. Do not replace them with manually authored long rows or silently partition by a different original field. Existing successful raw-field facet behavior must remain unchanged.

A row-preserving `createTimeUnitData` followed by an explicit derived-data facet already works; the unresolved boundary is the cardinality-changing fold. No new public option name is assumed by this issue. Supporting this requires a concrete dependency/dataflow design and the repository's explicit approval before core implementation.

## Validation needed

Verify parent and child raw/derived tables, exact per-panel marks and key order, descendant replay, immutable failure, edits/rematerialization, shared guides/scales, SVG/PDF/PNG consumers and stored facet contracts. Retain strict ambiguity and unsupported-dependency errors outside the approved boundary.

This blocked a real two-panel translation before any model request. The affected source is held; other chart work continues.

## Problem

The binned heatmap wrapper rejects the documented complete-axis `title: false` option before forwarding it to the shared axis owner. This prevents suppressing a title while retaining its line, ticks, and labels.

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';
const p = chart()
  .createCanvas({ width: 500, height: 400, margin: 100 })
  .createData({ values: [{ x: 1, y: 2 }, { x: 2, y: 3 }] });
p.createHeatmap({
  x: 'x', y: 'y', bin: { bins: 2 },
  guides: { axes: { x: { title: false } }, legend: false }
});
```

Actual: `createHeatmap binned axis title must be a plain object.`
Expected: retain the X-axis line, ticks and labels; omit its title. Omitted and explicit title objects should keep their existing behavior.

The same preprocessing helper also rejects the named-axis arrays approved for the pending0.0.19 Cartesian facade contract. It should forward each named entry through the same title handling, leaving closed-schema, empty-array, ID, and side validation to the shared axis owner.

## Fix and verification

Implementation checkpoint44aac78e preserves false and maps named entries without changing the public API. Eighteen focused tests cover X/Y/both title suppression, inferred/custom titles, full-axis omission, named owners, and atomic rejection of invalid inputs. Main integration is pending.

## Problem

A shared in-plot categorical legend can fit the complete facet plot union while being wider than one narrow cell. Existing public actions cannot currently express that lifecycle: creating the legend before `.facet()` validates it against the single-cell plot, while relocating a promoted outside legend afterward is unavailable on composition programs.

This differs from closed #56: requested outside positions now promote correctly. The blocked case cannot reach promotion with its intended in-plot position. It is also separate from #193, which concerns independent placement of selected legend blocks on one mark owner.

## Minimal reproduction

Tested on unreleased `0.0.17`, commit `690767d53415b93d22321c7915b0e2eddda24071`.

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';

const rows = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'].flatMap(panel =>
  ['Group one', 'Group two', 'Group three', 'Group four'].map(
    (category, i) => ({ panel, category, value: i + 1 })
  )
);

function base() {
  return chart()
    .createCanvas({
      width: 250, height: 350,
      margin: { left: 40, right: 150, top: 30, bottom: 45 }
    })
    .createData({ values: rows })
    .createBarPlot({ x: 'category', y: 'value', guides: false })
    .encodeColor({ field: 'category', layout: 'overlay' });
}

// Each inner plot is 60 × 275; four plots with 2px gaps span 246px.
const intended = base()
  .createLegend({
    position: 'top-left',
    titleStyle: { fontSize: 11 }, labels: { fontSize: 10 }
  }) // throws before facet: legend does not fit the 60px single-cell plot
  .facet({
    field: 'panel', spacing: 'plot',
    guides: { axes: 'outer', legend: 'shared' }
  })
  .editCompositionLayout({ gap: 2 });
```

An existing outside-first route also stops:

```js
const assembled = base()
  .createLegend({
    position: 'right',
    titleStyle: { fontSize: 11 }, labels: { fontSize: 10 }
  })
  .facet({
    field: 'panel', spacing: 'plot',
    guides: { axes: 'outer', legend: 'shared' }
  })
  .editCompositionLayout({ gap: 2 });

assembled.editLegendLayout({ position: 'top-left' });
// Error: editLegendLayout is not available on a composition ChartProgram.
```

A free `at` anchor before composition retains the same single-plot containment requirement. Enlarging the child inner plots changes the source design; reducing fonts, dropping categories or moving this legend outside also changes the intended result.

## Required behavior / scope for design review

Provide a public lifecycle for one shared legend positioned relative to the final facet plot union, while preserving each cell's explicit inner dimensions and checking the final occupied legend bounds. Keep normal single-unit containment strict and preserve existing omitted/default behavior. This report deliberately does not select or implement a new API shape.

Actual translation preflight `vl_0526` has 16 raw rows, four 60×275 panels, a 246px plot union and one original shared top-left legend. Its source SVG/data/compiled inputs remain frozen; only this candidate is held. No model request or chart-pixel inspection was needed for this diagnosis.

Enlarged rotated y-axis titles can pass native Canvas validation while their SVG font ink extends beyond the left edge. The inferred offset clamps the estimated one-em text box to the Canvas; a middle-baseline glyph can extend beyond that box. Increasing the left margin does not move the clamped title anchor.

### Reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

chart()
  .createCanvas({
    width: 451, height: 300,
    margin: { left: 51, top: 20, right: 60, bottom: 80 }
  })
  .createData({ values: [{ x: 0, y: 0 }, { x: 100, y: 140 }] })
  .createScatterPlot({ x: "x", y: "y", guides: false })
  .createAxes({
    x: { title: false },
    y: { title: { text: "Vertical total", fontSize: 14, fontWeight: 700 } }
  });
```

At revision `c53d1edda3625376b75f3157f798a3b099e1a750`, browser SVG bounds report `left: -2.28125` for font size 14 and `left: -3.21875` for size 18. Both calls succeed.

### Fix and validation

Fixed on branch [`fix/inferred-axis-title-font-bounds`](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/commit/7ab7388e): enlarged, rotated y titles reserve a quarter-em ink allowance when inferred placement reaches the Canvas edge. Explicit offsets, default-size typography, x titles, and spacious preferred placement retain their behavior. No public API is added.

The same browser probes report positive left bounds of `1.21875` and `1.28125`. Regression tests cover tight margins, ordinary preferred placement, explicit-offset parity, font edits, resize, persistence, immutability, and mirrored y-axis positions. Full unit 3,052, contracts 533, documentation 120, package validation, and installed-package consumer checks pass.

**Status:** fixed on the feature branch; stable integration and release remain pending. This is not a published npm fix.

## Problem

The existing categorical `editLegendBlock({ labelMap })` contract permits `label: ""` to hide only the displayed text while retaining its category and symbol. The materializer instead leaves an empty text primitive. Building succeeds, but actual SVG/Canvas/PNG rendering fails:

```text
Graphic "colorLegendLabels:0" requires a non-empty text property.
```

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";
import { renderToSVG } from "ggaction/svg";

const program = chart()
  .createCanvas({ width: 600, height: 500, margin: 160 })
  .createData({ values: [
    { x: 1, y: 2, group: "" },
    { x: 2, y: 3, group: "A" }
  ] })
  .createScatterPlot({ x: "x", y: "y", guides: false })
  .encodeColor({ field: "group" })
  .createLegend({ title: false })
  .editLegendBlock({
    target: "scatterPlot",
    channel: "color",
    labelMap: [{ value: "", label: "" }]
  });

renderToSVG(program);
```

Expected: two categorical symbols remain in their original item slots, only `A` has a text graphic, and all renderers succeed. Hiding every label must also preserve symbols and valid lane layout.

## Implemented correction

Feature checkpoint [21a2ad91](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/commit/21a2ad91a3f333dce199b81da89fa490fca94579) filters empty text items using original categorical layout indices and handles empty label collections in the existing lane owner. Domain/order/sample identity remain unchanged. There is no new public API, persisted schema, renderer workaround, or hidden whitespace label.

Validation: focused23/23; unit2977/2977; contracts532/532; docs120/120; all11 legend families render through actual SVG/PNG. Regression coverage includes four edges, all-empty maps, reset, drawing order, immutable inputs, persistence, resize, shared facet/repeat replay, and installed package consumers.

The affected translation family remains held until the feature checkpoint is integrated into the pinned runtime and normally reverified. Existing source, reviewed model code, failed attempts and token usage are retained. This checkpoint has **not** been published to npm or deployed to the documentation site.

## Problem

With a categorical color legend and a sampled opacity legend on the same point layer, `editLegendLabels` and `editLegendTitle` update only the categorical block. The calls succeed, while the opacity block retains its previous text styles. The optional `target` identifies the shared mark layer, so it cannot select the opacity block.

Observed on commit `0951a880733b912cf7c190a5d6f134cc4ff7d4bd`; no pixels or model calls are needed to reproduce.

## Public API reproduction

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';

const base = chart()
  .createCanvas({
    width: 700, height: 600,
    margin: { left: 50, top: 50, right: 450, bottom: 350 }
  })
  .createData({ values: [
    { x: 1, y: 1, g: 'A', a: 0 },
    { x: 2, y: 2, g: 'B', a: 1 }
  ] })
  .createScatterPlot({ x: 'x', y: 'y', guides: false })
  .encodeColor({ field: 'g' })
  .encodeOpacity({
    field: 'a', fieldType: 'quantitative',
    scale: { domain: [0, 1], range: [0.3, 0.8] }
  })
  .createLegend({ channels: ['opacity'] })
  .createLegend({ channels: ['color'] });

const labels = base.editLegendLabels({ fontSize: 10 });
const title = base.editLegendTitle({ fontSize: 11, fontWeight: 700 });
```

Inspecting the concrete guide configs gives:

| Program | Color labels/title/weight | Opacity labels/title/weight |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `base` | 12 / 13 / 600 | 12 / 13 / 600 |
| `labels` | **10** / 13 / 600 | 12 / 13 / 600 |
| `title` | 12 / **11 / 700** | 12 / 13 / 600 |

Using `target: 'scatterPlot'` does not disambiguate these blocks.

## Cause and contract question

The focused wrappers in `src/actions/guides/legends/focused.js` call `editLegend`. In `src/actions/guides/legends/edit.js`, its categorical branch returns immediately through `editCategorical`, passing only a companion size config. The later opacity fallback is never reached when a categorical block exists on the same layer.

The current documentation says focused editors have the same target inference as `editLegend`, and combined categorical/size typography has a special sharing policy. Therefore, changing every focused edit to update all blocks would need an explicit compatibility decision; this report does **not** propose silently changing independent-block styling.

Please provide an unambiguous documented way for focused text editors to address the opacity block (or clearly reject ambiguous edits with actionable guidance), and cover categorical+opacity and categorical+size+opacity coexistence. Preserve mark encodings, samples, other blocks, layout ownership, and complete-wrapper/focused-editor equivalence.

## Dataset impact and current workaround

Found while expressing the same charts at progressively finer action granularity (`vl_1728`, `vl_1724`). Replacing the complete opacity wrapper with focused edits changed actual opacity text from 10/11/700 to 12/13/600; exact rendered-family validation caught it. Keeping the complete opacity wrapper preserves the design and is the current workaround. No library implementation or runtime default was changed as part of this report.

## Problem

The public data-expression vocabulary cannot currently extract a calendar-year integer or a substring from a date field. This blocks an ordinary duration chart whose category is the difference between two calendar years. Parsing dates and subtracting timestamps does not preserve this meaning because elapsed milliseconds include month/day offsets and leap years.

For example, an item issued on `1986-12-30` and redeemed on `2024-07-17` belongs to calendar-year duration `38`. The source date strings must remain intact and the transformation should have ordinary derived-data provenance.

## Confirmed current behavior

Tested against unreleased `0.0.17`, commit `0951a880733b912cf7c190a5d6f134cc4ff7d4bd`:

```js
const source = chart().createData({
  id: "source",
  values: [{ issued: "1986-12-30", redeemed: "2024-07-17" }]
});

// All three are rejected by the existing computed-expression validator:
// { op: "year", operand: { field: "redeemed" } }
// { op: "datePart", part: "year", operand: { field: "redeemed" } }
// { op: "slice", operand: { field: "redeemed" }, start: 0, end: 4 }
```

Each failed operation leaves the source program unchanged. `createTimeUnitData({field:"redeemed",unit:"year",as:"yearStart"})` is useful bucketing, but its materialized result is the January 1 timestamp `1704067200000`, not the integer `2024`. It therefore does not substitute for year extraction. Dividing epoch timestamps by an average year length is not an exact alternative.

The installed `ComputedExpression` union contains parsing, numeric conversion, arithmetic/comparison, Boolean/coalescing, concatenation, and conditional expressions; it has no calendar-component or substring operator.

## Possible directions, pending API design review

Either a typed date-component expression with an explicit UTC/time-zone policy, or a typed substring expression followed by existing numeric conversion, would cover this use case. A date-component operator is preferable when input already represents dates; substring semantics are independently useful for source-authored string transformations.

This issue records a capability gap, not approval of a particular public schema. No API implementation has been made.

## Acceptance criteria

- Compute the exact integer calendar years and the difference `38` for the example.
- Specify supported inputs, UTC/time-zone behavior, null/invalid-value handling, and range validation.
- Preserve raw rows, derived-data lineage, immutability, serialization, and replay.
- Cover leap dates, year boundaries, dates before 1970, and invalid inputs with regression tests.
- Update public declarations, contracts, and documentation together; keep arbitrary JavaScript callbacks unnecessary.

The affected translation is stopped and retained with its original data and all attempt costs; other charts continue.

## Problem

`createData({ values })` cannot preserve otherwise valid object rows containing an unused empty-string key, a common result of a CSV with an unnamed index/header column. Failure happens before a chart can select its valid named x/y fields.

This is a **stored-row/schema contract decision**, not a request to let chart encodings reference empty field names. No implementation is proposed as implicitly approved.

## Verified environment and reproduction

ggaction commit `0951a880733b912cf7c190a5d6f134cc4ff7d4bd`, Node 22.23.1:

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const rows = [{ x: 1, y: 2, "": "unreferenced CSV cell" }];

chart().createData({ values: rows });
// Dataset schema field 2 name must be a non-empty string.

chart().createData({
  values: rows,
  schema: { fields: [
    { name: "x", storageType: "number" },
    { name: "y", storageType: "number" }
  ] }
});
// createData row 0 contains undeclared field "".

chart().createData({
  values: rows,
  schema: { fields: [
    { name: "x", storageType: "number" },
    { name: "y", storageType: "number" },
    { name: "", storageType: "string" }
  ] }
});
// Dataset schema field 2 name must be a non-empty string.
```

All three failures are atomic: the earlier program and caller-owned rows remain unchanged. The rows need not be rendered to reproduce this boundary.

## Current contract path

- [`createData`](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/0951a880733b912cf7c190a5d6f134cc4ff7d4bd/src/actions/data/create.js) infers or normalizes a schema and validates rows before storing them.
- [`datasetSchema.js`](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/0951a880733b912cf7c190a5d6f134cc4ff7d4bd/src/grammar/datasetSchema.js#L51-L60) normalizes every inferred/declared schema field through `requireStringValue`; the imported validator explicitly requires a non-empty string.
- Declaring a schema that omits the unused column is not an escape hatch: [`validateRowsAgainstSchema`](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/0951a880733b912cf7c190a5d6f134cc4ff7d4bd/src/grammar/datasetSchema.js#L149-L158) rejects undeclared raw keys.
- [`CORE.md` createData contract](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/0951a880733b912cf7c190a5d6f134cc4ff7d4bd/agent_docs/contract/current/CORE.md#L300-L319) describes preservation/ownership of plain-object rows; it does not currently explain this raw-key limitation. The schema validator nevertheless makes the restriction explicit in executable behavior.

## Impact

A current fixed-source translation selection has 33 of 400 raw datasets with a `""` key. Some contain an unused row index; others contain empty cells or a nonempty unused value. The charts reference ordinary named fields. A lossless workflow cannot silently delete or rename these cells just to satisfy schema inference.

We have paused only those affected sources while retaining every raw source, resolved data file, request, failure and usage record. Replacement sources can keep the batch moving. This issue does not classify the original data as syntactically invalid or count failed translations as accepted.

## Decision needed before implementation

Choose and document a lossless input policy. Possible designs include:

1. Preserve empty raw keys in stored rows/schema while keeping chart-facing field references non-empty. This requires an explicit decision about the persisted schema invariant and how inspection, transforms, revision and replay retain those cells.
2. Provide an explicit lossless source adapter with a recorded original-key/normalized-key mapping. Renaming must be visible and reversible; caller data and raw artifacts remain unchanged.
3. Retain the rejection as an intentional limitation and document it clearly, including the absence of a declared-schema bypass.

Do not silently discard unknown cells or weaken all identifier validation as an incidental fix. No new public option is requested without reviewing its data ownership and persistence semantics.

## Validation for any chosen capability

- Empty and whitespace keys, unused index cells, meaningful nonempty cells, and ordinary named fields.
- Complete raw-row/cell preservation with caller input and prior immutable programs unchanged.
- Declared and inferred schemas, empty datasets, revision, derived transforms, serialization/replay and facets.
- Encoding references continue to enforce the approved field-name rules.
- No changes to existing normal-key chart geometry, scales or rendering.
- Public types, input-boundary tests, current contracts and docs agree on the chosen policy.

Deduplication searches for `empty field name` and `blank column` found no matching issue.

## Problem

A quantitative point-size legend cannot show display labels that differ from its numeric sample values. `editLegendBlock({ target, channel: "size", labelMap })` is present in the public type, but its implementation rejects sampled continuous size blocks: `editLegendBlock labelMap requires a categorical block.`

This prevents preserving both correct glyph geometry and meaningful raw size labels after a numeric area normalization. Changing the size scale back to the original raw-value domain changes the marks; hiding the legend or drawing a manual legend would lose the intended domain-level authoring flow.

## Verified contract

Reproduced with ggaction commit `0951a880733b912cf7c190a5d6f134cc4ff7d4bd` and Node 22.23.1.

- [`docs/api/legends/editing.md`](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/0951a880733b912cf7c190a5d6f134cc4ff7d4bd/docs/api/legends/editing.md#L229-L243) documents exact continuous samples through `values`, and describes `labelMap` only for categorical blocks.
- [`types/program.d.ts`](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/0951a880733b912cf7c190a5d6f134cc4ff7d4bd/types/program.d.ts#L4646-L4676) exposes direct `labelMap`; the `text` patch contains typography/color only. `text.labelMap` is not supported.
- [`src/actions/guides/legends/blocks.js`](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/0951a880733b912cf7c190a5d6f134cc4ff7d4bd/src/actions/guides/legends/blocks.js#L127-L134) explicitly restricts the map to categorical descriptors. A second sampled-block guard exists later in the same module.

## Source-neutral reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const rows = [
  { x: 0, y: 1, rawSize: 0, area: Math.PI },
  { x: 1, y: 2, rawSize: 2, area: 65.44984694978736 },
  { x: 2, y: 3, rawSize: 4, area: 127.75810124598492 }
];

const p = chart()
  .createCanvas({
    width: 500,
    height: 300,
    margin: { left: 40, top: 40, right: 160, bottom: 40 }
  })
  .createData({ values: rows })
  .createScatterPlot({
    x: { field: "x", scale: { domain: [0, 2] } },
    y: { field: "y", scale: { domain: [0, 3] } },
    guides: false
  })
  .encodeSize({
    field: "area",
    fieldType: "quantitative",
    scale: { domain: [0, 361], range: [0, 361] }
  })
  .createLegend({
    channels: ["size"],
    values: [Math.PI, 65.44984694978736, 127.75810124598492],
    title: "rawSize"
  });

p.editLegendBlock({
  target: "scatterPlot",
  channel: "size",
  labelMap: [
    { value: Math.PI, label: "0" },
    { value: 65.44984694978736, label: "2" },
    { value: 127.75810124598492, label: "4" }
  ]
});
// Throws: editLegendBlock labelMap requires a categorical block.
```

The failed call leaves the previous immutable program unchanged; this was checked against its complete semantic and graphic state. No pixel inspection is needed to reproduce the boundary.

## Why the raw labels and geometry differ

In the blocking imported design, the raw continuous size field spans 0–9. Its graphical area before shape normalization is `4 + (361 - 4) / 9 * rawSize`. Different circle-area conventions require the circle rows to multiply that area by `Math.PI / 4`, while square/triangle/cross rows retain their original area. The derived `area` field and an identity area scale preserve the actual mixed-shape glyphs.

The reader should still see legend labels `0, 2, 4, 6, 8`. For circle legend samples their numeric geometry values are approximately `3.14159, 65.44985, 127.75810, 190.06636, 252.37461`. An ordinary numeric format cannot express this inverse mapping. Replacing the identity scale with a raw-value scale corrupts already-correct plot geometry. Categorical size encoding is a different semantic choice and does not supply the continuous sampled values needed here.

## Small API proposal, for review

Extend the existing direct `editLegendBlock({ target, channel, labelMap })` behavior to sampled size blocks. This needs no additional top-level option or rendering primitive. Treat it as a **display-only typed lookup over exact numeric sample values**:

- Preserve the size field, scale domain/range, sample values/order, symbol areas and point geometry.
- Require finite numeric map keys for sampled size blocks.
- Unmapped samples keep their normal numeric formatter; `"auto"` removes the display map.
- Reject duplicate keys, extra entry fields and malformed labels atomically; do not mutate caller data or previous programs.
- Retain the map across legend layout/text edits and data/scale rematerialization. Define behavior when a sample is removed or reintroduced.
- Keep block targeting unambiguous when size coexists with color/shape legends, including shared/faceted replay.

Initial scope can be size-only; whether sampled opacity/stroke-width should share this behavior is an explicit follow-up design choice. This issue records the capability need and proposed semantics; it does not imply approval to implement a new public contract.

## Validation expected

1. Exact custom samples with labels `0/2/4`, and missing-map fallback.
2. Before/after point geometry, size scale and legend symbol geometry equality; only legend text and its necessary layout bounds change.
3. `"auto"` reset, sample changes, repeated edits, serialization/replay and supported facets.
4. All supported legend edges, label measurement and clipping/collision checks.
5. Invalid maps fail atomically with caller input unchanged.
6. Types, action cards, contracts and public docs describe the supported sampled-size behavior.

Related but distinct: #155 added **categorical** size encodings; #161 added bounded overflow for **categorical** size legends. Neither covers custom labels on continuous samples. Searches for `labelMap`, `legend label`, `size legend`, and `labelMap sampled size` found no duplicate issue.

## Problem

A layered chart with a scatter layer and a data-bound text annotation layer renders before faceting, but `facet()` rejects the text layer:

```
Error: facet does not support mark "text" of type text.
    at classifyPrimaryLayer
    at resolveFacetFamily
    at resolveFacetDefinition
```

This blocks static translation of Vega-Lite faceted plots that place a label inside each panel, such as a cluster area or sample count.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const rows = [
  { group: "A", x: 0, y: 0, label: "area=10" },
  { group: "A", x: 1, y: 1 },
  { group: "B", x: 0, y: 0, label: "area=20" },
  { group: "B", x: 1, y: 1 }
];

export function buildChart() {
  return chart()
    .createCanvas()
    .createData({ id: "rows", values: rows })
    .createScatterPlot({
      data: "rows",
      x: { field: "x" },
      y: { field: "y" }
    })
    .filterData({
      id: "labels",
      source: "rows",
      field: "y",
      predicate: { op: "eq", value: 0 }
    })
    .createTextMark({ data: "labels", align: "center", baseline: "middle" })
    .encodeChannels({
      target: "text",
      channels: {
        x: { field: "x" },
        y: { field: "y" },
        text: { field: "label" }
      }
    })
    .facet({ field: "group", data: "rows", columns: 2 });
}
```

Tested with ggaction `0.0.17`.

## Expected behavior

`facet()` should clone or partition compatible data-bound text layers together with the primary Cartesian layer. The text should reuse the panel's x/y scales and receive the same facet filter.

## Why this needs public support

Dropping the text changes the chart's explicitly authored per-panel annotation. Precomputing separate child charts would greatly increase the number of actions and bypass the high-level facet API. Drawing text outside ggaction would also violate the public-API-only translation constraint.

## Suggested acceptance test

1. Build the reproduction without an exception.
2. Produce two facet panels.
3. Render one point series and one label in each panel.
4. Preserve shared/outer guide behavior when `guides: { axes: "outer" }` is supplied.

## Problem

ggaction 0.0.17 rejects a categorical legend placed on the right when `columns` is greater than one. This prevents compact static translations with many categories; a 21-item year legend that fits in three columns becomes an excessively tall single column or must move to a different side.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

export function buildChart() {
  return chart()
    .createCanvas({ width: 620, height: 380, margin: { top: 20, right: 300, bottom: 60, left: 70 } })
    .createData({ values: [
      { x: 1, y: 2, year: "1987" },
