## Enhancement

Offer opt-in robust residual reweighting for LOESS, retaining the current ordinary local fit as the default. The existing [regression documentation](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/e539c0b71be569dfc5d82f4180e8a08a8f580c76/docs/api/regression.md#L210) explicitly states that robust LOESS is unsupported; this request extends that documented capability boundary. It is not a claim that ordinary LOESS is mathematically incorrect or that identical method names promise identical estimators.

## Why it matters

Static translations from a robust smoother can change substantially if passed directly to `createRegressionPlot({method:'loess',span:...})`. In one real chart the first fitted point changed by51.1 units. We preserve its already-resolved source fit as data and use `createLinePlot`, which is valid for the frozen initial chart. Native robust estimation would retain the statistical operation and work when input data changes.

## Independent synthetic reproduction

Executed with ggaction0.0.17, vega-statistics1.9.0 and Node22.23.1. ggaction source pinned to `e539c0b71be569dfc5d82f4180e8a08a8f580c76`.

```js
import {chart} from 'ggaction';
import {regressionLoess} from 'vega-statistics';
const rows = Array.from({length:20}, (_,x) => ({
  x, y:0.2*x*x + (x===2 ? 80 : 0)
}));
const p = chart().createCanvas().createData({values:rows})
 .createRegressionPlot({x:'x',y:'y',method:'loess',span:.5,guides:false});
const robust = regressionLoess(rows,d=>d.x,d=>d.y,.5);
console.log(p.semanticSpec.datasets[1].values[0],robust[0]);
```

At x=0:

- ggaction:17.964434500763524
- Vega:-4.676572324762528
- maximum absolute difference across the20 returned positions:22.641006825526052

Both use10 neighbors here, so this fixture avoids floor-vs-ceiling neighbor-count differences. The first ordinary fit is affected by the outlier; the comparator applies residual-based robust weights. The comparator is evidence of a different estimator, not a universal oracle for what LOESS must mean.

## Implementation and API considerations

[`fitLoessGroup`](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/e539c0b71be569dfc5d82f4180e8a08a8f580c76/src/grammar/regression/models.js#L379) currently applies one tricube-weighted local linear fit. Consider a documented robustness/iteration option with residual reweighting, stopping tolerance and behavior for near-zero median residuals. Define neighbor rounding, duplicate-x handling and prediction positions separately; they can also affect cross-library parity.

Preserve stable ordinary defaults, replay/serialization, grouping, `editRegression`, and `createRegressionPlot` forwarding. Do not imply that adding robust iterations also adds LOESS confidence bands.

Suggested tests: zero iterations reproduces current results; curved/noisy data with an outlier; exact linear data with zero median residual; duplicates; small spans and groups; edits and predictions. Document estimator choices and numerical tolerances.

This is non-blocking for our fixed initial-render workflow because frozen resolved fitted values plus a public line facade preserve the source result.

## Problem

The public polar frame restricts an explicit pixel radius to the nearest edge of its plot bounds. A valid static source chart can intentionally draw arcs beyond that frame. Preserving both its explicit 250×350 plotting area and 160px outer radius is currently impossible with the tested public pie/arc APIs.

This blocks one chart in a 150-chart Vega-Lite translation batch. We stopped that chart and continued the others; changing its authored radius to 125px or changing its authored plot width to 320px would alter the requested geometry.

## Verified environment and reproduction

npm `ggaction@0.0.17`, Node22.23.1, official commit `e539c0b71be569dfc5d82f4180e8a08a8f580c76` (also GitHub main when checked on2026-09-16).

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';
chart()
  .createCanvas({
    width: 330,
    height: 470,
    margin: { top: 50, right: 40, bottom: 70, left: 40 }
  })
  .createData({ values: [
    { category: 'A', value: 75 },
    { category: 'B', value: 25 }
  ] })
  .createPiePlot({
    category: 'category', value: 'value', aggregate: 'sum', guides: false
  })
  .editCoordinate({
    target: 'polar',
    polarFrame: { radius: { unit: 'px', value: 160 } }
  });
```

Actual, independently executed: `Polar frame radius 160 exceeds the maximum radius 125.`

The inner bounds are250×350. The intended circle is centered at their center, diameter320, extending35px past each horizontal edge. This is intentional mark overflow; the outer canvas already has room. It is not a negative-data-radius or nonfinite-input case.

## Investigated alternatives

- [`resolvePolarFrame`](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/e539c0b71be569dfc5d82f4180e8a08a8f580c76/src/grammar/polar.js#L66) calculates the maximum as the nearest plot edge and rejects larger pixel radii. Moving the center only reduces this maximum.
- Proportional arcs use the frame's available radius for the outer radius in [`arcs.js`](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/e539c0b71be569dfc5d82f4180e8a08a8f580c76/src/grammar/arcs.js#L140).
- Explicit radial ranges and mapped radii are also bounded in `polar.js` (`validateRadialRange` and `polarToCartesian`). An r encoding is not an escape hatch for proportional quantitative theta.
- We found no public independent arc outerRadius or overflow option. We did not modify the library or use arbitrary SVG drawing.

## Requested capability

Provide an explicit opt-in for polar mark overflow, or separate the pixel radius geometry from plot clipping. Keep the current fit-within-bounds behavior as the default. The public option spelling is a design choice.

Preserve semantic frame bounds and center, explicit radius, resizing/replay behavior, and guide/layout ownership. Define clipping and export bounds explicitly so the overflow does not disappear from SVG/PNG output.

Suggested tests: default-fit unchanged; explicit in-bounds radius; opt-in160px radius on250×350 frame; off-center frame; invalid/nonfinite radii still rejected; edits and serialization retain the policy. A standalone two-slice example should render with the same proportions and authored radius without increasing the plotting area.

## Environment and scope

Verified with npm `ggaction@0.0.17`, Node 22.23.1. GitHub `main` resolved to `e539c0b71be569dfc5d82f4180e8a08a8f580c76` on 2026-09-16, the same commit as our pinned official source snapshot. These are authoring improvements found while translating 50 static Vega-Lite charts (47 accepted); they are not new blockers for those accepted charts. We prioritize preserved meaning, visual fidelity, then fewer public actions and explicit options. Synthetic data below is independent of the dataset.

## Problem

Axis/title wrapping does not provide multiline data text. Text mark options lack a line-height/break contract. Translating three rows containing one, two or three lines currently needs three text layers, three encoding actions and null padding, even though it is one logical text mark per row.

## Executed reproduction

```js
import {chart} from 'ggaction';
import {renderToSVG} from 'ggaction/svg';
const base=()=>chart().createCanvas().createData({values:[
 {x:1,y:2,t:'first\nsecond'}, {x:3,y:4,t:'one line'}
]});
const p=base().createTextMark({id:'labels'})
 .encodeChannels({target:'labels',channels:{
   x:{field:'x'},y:{field:'y'},text:{field:'t'}
 }});
console.log(renderToSVG(p));
// One <text> node containing "first\nsecond", no separate line layout/tspan.
base().createTextMark({lineHeight:14});
// Unknown createTextMark option "lineHeight".
```

The current [TextMarkOptions](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/e539c0b71be569dfc5d82f4180e8a08a8f580c76/types/program.d.ts#L3787) and [SVG text rendering](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/e539c0b71be569dfc5d82f4180e8a08a8f580c76/src/renderers/svg.js#L428) do not define a multiline block. Newlines in a plain SVG text node do not create separate positioned lines.

## Requested contract

Define explicit line splitting (newline and/or array of strings), line height, alignment and block baseline. For the translation case, center each line horizontally and allow the first line to stay centered on the data point, with subsequent lines 13px below; block-middle anchoring should be a distinct documented choice.

Make this available to data text and source-owned labels where appropriate. Preserve the distinction between missing text and intentionally empty lines. Reuse measured text infrastructure rather than implementing only SVG tspan output.

## Acceptance checks

- Rows with 1, 2 and 3 lines produce one logical mark each, with exact requested line spacing.
- Alignment, rotation, collision bounds, clipping and overflow use the entire block.
- SVG and Canvas agree; glyph fallback and explicit fonts are retained.
- Old single-line strings and null handling remain compatible.

This would replace repeated text layers in accepted charts; it is an authoring improvement, not a claim that their current workaround is invalid.

