## Problem

Raw interval bars already work through `createBarMark` + `encodeXRange`/`encodeYRange` + the independent position and width actions. The bar facade requires a single field on both axes and cannot represent those same raw bounds. Five layered interval bars in a bullet chart require 17 public calls just for the marks/encodings; a facade could own each interval without changing semantics.

## Executed reproduction

```js
import {chart} from 'ggaction';
const base=()=>chart().createCanvas().createData({values:[
 {k:'A',lo:0,hi:2}, {k:'B',lo:1,hi:4}
]});
base().createBarPlot({x:'k',y:{lower:'lo',upper:'hi'},guides:false});
// createBarPlot y field must be a non-empty string.

// Existing supported equivalent:
base().createBarMark()
 .encodeYRange({lower:'lo',upper:'hi'})
 .encodeX({field:'k',fieldType:'ordinal'});
// succeeds and renders both raw intervals
```

[Facade implementation](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/e539c0b71be569dfc5d82f4180e8a08a8f580c76/src/actions/charts/bar.js) normalizes x/y with `normalizeFieldEncoding` and calls only `encodeX`/`encodeY`.

## Proposal

Allow a clearly discriminated lower/upper position channel (or a separate generic interval-bar facade) that delegates to the existing range encoding. Support both orientations, pixel/band widths, appearance and guide policy. Keep raw row intervals distinct from aggregation, stacking and statistical interval estimation.

A range bar should also compose with a raw line/point on an explicitly shared compatible value scale. Our layered bar+line workaround uses a raw zero-to-value range because changing it to an ordinary aggregated bar introduces incompatible measure policy. Do not silently introduce mean/sum or overwrite an unrelated layer's scale.

## Acceptance checks

- Horizontal and vertical nonzero lower bounds reproduce low-level bar geometry.
- Five bullet layers with distinct widths and a target rule need one facade per bar, while preserving explicit data ownership, render order, independent facet scales and guide behavior.
- Shared scales and resize/rematerialization preserve all consumers and original rows.
- Unknown or ambiguous range/field combinations fail clearly.

Reducing 17 calls to five is a design target, not a measured result of an implemented API. Existing code was optimized only with current supported calls.

