  .encodeGroup({field:"g"}).encodeStrokeWidth({field:"m"})
  .createLegend({channels:["strokeWidth"]});
p.editLegend({title:"This title is much wider than the available right margin"});
p.editCanvas({height:100});
```

이전 rematerializer는 plot 오른쪽+30, titleY=plot.y+28, sampleY=titleY+34+i*32를 직접 기록하고 text/stroke occupied bounds를 검사하지 않습니다. 기존 close-numeric-label 회귀 역시 16자리 labels와 right margin200으로 범위를 넘기는 출력을 허용했습니다. 다른 continuous 범례의 명시적인 margin error 계약과 불일치합니다.

## 기대 동작 및 수정 방향

공통 item layout으로 네 edge의 좌표를 계산하고, 실제 각 sample stroke extent와 visible text/background를 Canvas에 대해 검증합니다. 숨긴 title은 측정에서 제외합니다. 생성·편집·resize가 동일한 최종 상태에 대해 동일한 결과를 내고, 거부된 요청은 원본 프로그램/trace를 변경하지 않아야 합니다. 기존 긴 숫자 label의 distinct-format 검사는 충분한 margin fixture로 유지하고 overflow 자체를 별도 회귀로 검증합니다.

## Reproduction

At commit `195ea2053df0127a14ac2759b4a5fb16a6ee17ad`, automatic categorical legend recipes depend on authoring order and retain a removed companion line.

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';
const base = chart().createCanvas({ width: 800, height: 600, margin: { right: 250 } })
  .createData({ values: [
    { x: 1, y: 2, g: 'A' }, { x: 2, y: 3, g: 'A' },
    { x: 1, y: 3, g: 'B' }, { x: 2, y: 4, g: 'B' }
  ] })
  .createPointMark({ id: 'points' }).encodeX({ field: 'x' }).encodeY({ field: 'y' })
  .encodeColor({ field: 'g' }).encodeShape({ field: 'g' });
const addLine = p => p.createLineMark({ id: 'lines' })
  .encodeX({ field: 'x' }).encodeY({ field: 'y' })
  .encodeGroup({ field: 'g' }).encodeColor({ field: 'g' });
const early = addLine(base.createLegend({ target: 'points' }));
const late = addLine(base).createLegend({ target: 'points' });
const removed = late.removeMark({ target: 'lines' });
```

Actual `guideConfigs.legend.series.symbol.layers` types:
- `early`: `["point"]`
- `late`: `["line", "point"]`
- `removed`: `["line", "point"]`

Expected: early and late both use `["line", "point"]`; removed uses `["point"]`. The mark/color-scale context is equivalent, and `inferredSymbol:true` means the recipe is automatic. A caller-specified recipe must remain unchanged instead.

## Cause and impact

`resolveLegendSymbol` considers compatible companion lines during creation, explicit auto reset, and categorical content revision. Ordinary `rematerializeLegend` updates domain, title and scales but does not resolve or reconcile the automatic symbol recipe. Users get different legends for equivalent authoring orders, and a legend can advertise a line that no longer exists.

The fix should use the same recipe owner during dependency-driven replay, reconcile obsolete/new symbol components and their drawing order, preserve explicit recipes, and retain title visibility/style/order. Regression coverage should include Full/Basic creation order, line removal and encoding rebinding, Canvas/data/scale replay, and renderer/package evidence.

## 재현 기준

`4d7e37d41713c5a9d6ed51959fcf0e01f2b75a01` (Roadmap 6 개발 브랜치). Full API로 단독 size 범례는 생성되지만 generic/focused editor가 모두 실패합니다.

```javascript
import { chart } from "ggaction";
const p = chart().createCanvas({ width: 700, height: 500, margin: { right: 220 } })
  .createData({ values: [{ x: 1, y: 2, m: 10 }, { x: 2, y: 3, m: 20 }] })
  .createPointMark({ id: "points" }).encodeX({ field: "x" }).encodeY({ field: "y" })
  .encodeSize({ field: "m" }).createLegend({ channels: ["size"] });
p.editLegend({ count: 3 });
p.editLegendTitle({ title: "Magnitude" });
p.editLegendLabels({ fontWeight: 700 });
```

## 실제 결과와 원인

각 편집을 p에서 별도로 실행하면 `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'inferredTitle')` 또는 `reading 'target'` 예외가 발생합니다. `editLegend`가 categorical, interval, strokeWidth, gradient/opacity만 dispatch하고 size owner를 누락하여 undefined configuration으로 continuous editor를 호출합니다. `editLegendLayout({position:"left"})`처럼 아직 지원하지 않는 요청도 해당 기능의 설명 대신 같은 내부 예외를 냅니다.

Size rematerializer도 default text styles와 항상 존재하는 title graphic을 가정하므로 dispatch만 추가해서는 hidden/custom title, text style, replay를 보장할 수 없습니다.

## 기대 동작과 수정 범위

Standalone size의 count, title(custom/auto/false), labels/titleStyle와 기존 focused editor를 지원하고 값을 immutable recipe로 저장합니다. Canvas/scale/data replay에서 유지하고, 지원하지 않는 layout/symbol/border 옵션은 구체적인 오류로 원자적으로 거절합니다. 현재 right/default geometry는 보존하며 네 edge 확장은 후속 W2 변경으로 별도 검증합니다. Node/Basic boundary, exact equal-area radius, title restore, focused action hierarchy, 실패 불변성과 renderer/installed consumer를 검증합니다.

## 재현

기준 commit: `816ec99c`, Node 22.23.2.

```js
import { chart } from './src/index.js';
const p = chart()
  .createCanvas({ width: 600, height: 400, margin: 70 })
  .createData({ values: [{ a: 0, b: 10, c: 100 }, { a: 2, b: 20, c: 200 }] })
  .createParallelCoordinates({ id: 'lines', dimensions: ['a', 'b', 'c'], guides: { legend: false } });
const q = p.encodeParallelCoordinates({ target: 'lines', dimensions: ['c', 'b', 'a'] });
console.log(q.semanticSpec.layers[0].encoding.parallel.dimensions.map(d => d.field));
// ['c', 'b', 'a']
console.log(q.graphicSpec.objects.parallelAxisTitles.items.map(i => i.properties.text));
// ['a', 'b', 'c'] — 잘못된 이전 제목
```

위 결과를 실제 실행했습니다. Line paths와 scale domains는 새 dimension 순서를 따르지만 축 제목·tick/label graphic은 이전 데이터 해석을 표시합니다. Dimension을 추가하거나 제거하면 축 개수도 남아 있고 semantic axis.scales와 runtime config의 dependency 목록이 어긋납니다.

## 기대 결과와 원인

Dimension replacement 한 번으로 scales → marks → existing guides가 같은 최종 encoding을 반영해야 합니다. 축이 생략된 chart에는 축을 새로 만들면 안 되고, 다른 Parallel layer가 소유한 독립 axes도 변경하면 안 됩니다.

현재 encodeParallelCoordinates는 guide refresh를 끈 채 scale만 계산하고 line만 다시 그립니다. 이미 있는 shared materialization planner를 적용하면 영향을 받는 guide를 수집·중복 제거해 마지막에 한 번 갱신할 수 있습니다. Parallel axis rematerializer도 최종 dimension scale 목록을 semantic guide와 runtime dependency config에 함께 반영해야 합니다.

순서/제목/domain 교체, dimension 추가·축소, Canvas/scale replay, omitted axes, 다른 owner 보존 및 이전 program 불변성을 검사하고 수정 push 후 닫겠습니다.

## Summary

Several current documentation statements disagree with executable behavior at `fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6` (package version 0.0.12). These can make users and code-authoring agents reject supported combinations or calculate the wrong glyph dimensions.

## Verified discrepancies

| Location | Current statement | Verified behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Supported Features: Line position](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/docs/supported-features.md#L27) | Temporal x and aggregate y | `createLinePlot` also supports direct quantitative x/y |
| [Supported Features: Error bar position](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/docs/supported-features.md#L52) | Categorical/temporal independent axis | Explicit error bars also support a quantitative independent axis, implemented for #57 |
| [encodeRadius contract](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/agent_docs/contract/current/ENCODINGS.md#L1147) | A positive radius is a square's half-side | Radius is converted to area `PI * r²`; an equal-area square has side `sqrt(PI) * r` |
| [encodeOpacity contract](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/agent_docs/contract/current/ENCODINGS.md#L1115) | Optional point target; quantitative point field | Constant and field opacity also accept rule targets |

## Reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const source = () => chart().createCanvas().createData({
  values: [
    { x: 1, y: 2, c: 1, lower: 1, upper: 3 },
    { x: 2, y: 3, c: 2, lower: 2, upper: 4 }
  ]
});

const line = source().createLinePlot({ x: "x", y: "y", guides: false });
console.log(line.semanticSpec.layers[0].encoding);
// Both position field types are quantitative; no aggregate.

const interval = source().createErrorBar({
  x: { field: "x", fieldType: "quantitative" },
  y: { center: "y", lower: "lower", upper: "upper" },
  caps: true
});
console.log(interval.semanticSpec.layers[0].encoding.x.fieldType);
// "quantitative"

const square = source().createPointMark({ shape: "square" })
  .encodeX({ field: "x" }).encodeY({ field: "y" })
  .encodeRadius({ value: 3 });
console.log(square.graphicSpec.objects.point.items[0].properties.width);
// 5.317361552716548, not 6

const rules = source().createRuleMark({ id: "rules" })
  .encodeX({ field: "x", fieldType: "quantitative" })
  .encodeOpacity({ field: "c" });
console.log(rules.graphicSpec.objects.rules.items.map(item => item.properties.opacity));
// [0.2, 1]
```

## Expected correction

Describe the implemented support and units accurately, and synchronize the relevant public table/action descriptions with the owning current contracts. The radius behavior is intentional equal-area behavior already covered by [radius-encoding tests](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/test/unit/actions/encodings/radius-encoding.test.js); changing runtime geometry to match stale prose is not the requested correction.

The runtime rule target is explicit in [encodeOpacity](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/actions/encodings/appearance.js), and direct line/error-bar positions have current executable coverage.

## Validation gap

All generated-document/catalog checks passed during this audit. They validate generated metadata and structure but did not catch these stale handwritten restrictions or unit descriptions. Add a focused executable example or ownership check where it prevents recurrence, rather than treating successful generation alone as semantic documentation validation.

Filed during a repository audit at the maintainer's request.

## Summary

Line color and stroke-dash assignments fail if made before both position encodings, even though the same assignments succeed after x/y. A constant dash fails too. The equivalent `encodeGroup` action already supports pending line positions.

These appearance assignments do not require a complete path to store valid intent; eager materialization makes otherwise compatible authoring order affect success.

## Environment

Source `fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6`, package version 0.0.12, Node.js 22.23.1 on macOS arm64.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

function line() {
  return chart()
    .createCanvas()
    .createData({ values: [
      { x: 1, y: 1, g: "A" }, { x: 2, y: 2, g: "A" },
      { x: 3, y: 3, g: "B" }, { x: 4, y: 4, g: "B" }
    ] })
    .createLineMark();
}

for (const [method, options] of [
  ["encodeColor", { field: "g" }],
  ["encodeStrokeDash", { field: "g" }],
  ["encodeStrokeDash", { value: "dashed" }]
]) {
  try {
    line()[method](options)
      .encodeX({ field: "x" }).encodeY({ field: "y" });
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(method, error.message);
  }
  // Same final assignments, different order: succeeds.
  line().encodeX({ field: "x" }).encodeY({ field: "y" })
    [method](options);
}

// Control: another series assignment already supports pending positions.
line().encodeGroup({ field: "g" })
  .encodeX({ field: "x" }).encodeY({ field: "y" });
```

All three early appearance cases throw:

```text
Line mark "line" requires x and y scales.
```

## Expected behavior

Persist the validated appearance/series intent while the line is incomplete, then materialize it once position prerequisites are complete. Final semantic and graphic state should converge with the position-first order.

This follows the repository's [compatible authoring-order rule](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/actions/AGENTS.md#public-api-shape) and the existing `encodeGroup` behavior. It does not imply that missing required primary endpoints or incompatible bar/area grain should be accepted.

## Cause and regression coverage

- The line policy in [materialization/marks/policies.js](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/materialization/marks/policies.js) lacks a complete-only appearance guard.
- [getEncodingMaterializationStages](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/materialization/marks/index.js#L105) consequently schedules the line materializer eagerly.
- The constant branch of [encodeStrokeDash](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/actions/encodings/strokeDash.js#L91) calls it directly.
- [encodeGroup](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/actions/encodings/ranged.js#L282) already checks `canMaterializeLine`.

Test appearance before/between/after x/y, both field and constant dash, Cartesian/Polar/Parallel compatible paths, group/color/dash compatibility, immutability, and final geometry/trace consistency.

Filed during a repository audit at the maintainer's request. No source fix is included.

## Summary

Creating grid lines after label leaders places the grid above those leaders. A later canvas/scale rematerialization deletes and reinserts the leaders above the grid. The same final chart settings therefore produce different pixels depending on editing history.

## Environment

Source `fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6`, package version 0.0.12, Node.js 22.23.1 on macOS arm64. Verified in concrete child order and Canvas/PNG pixels.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const before = chart()
  .createCanvas({ width: 400, height: 300, margin: 30 })
  .createData({ values: [
    { x: 50, y: 50, label: "Alpha long label" },
    { x: 50, y: 51, label: "Beta long label" },
    { x: 51, y: 50, label: "Gamma long label" }
  ] })
  .createScatterPlot({
    id: "points",
    x: { field: "x", scale: { domain: [0, 100] } },
    y: { field: "y", scale: { domain: [0, 100] } },
    guides: false
  })
  .createTextMark({
    id: "labels", fontSize: 14, dx: 8, baseline: "middle"
  })
  .encodeText({ field: "label" })
  .layoutLabels({
    target: "labels", axis: "y", maxDisplacement: 70, padding: 4,
    leader: { stroke: "#ff0000", strokeWidth: 6 }
  })
  .createHorizontalGrid({
    scale: "y", values: [50, 51], color: "#00ff00", lineWidth: 8
  });

const after = before.editCanvas({ width: 400 });
console.log(before.graphicSpec.objects["plot-main"].children);
console.log(after.graphicSpec.objects["plot-main"].children);
```

## Actual results

Before:

```text
labels-label-leaders, horizontalGridLines, points, labels
```

After the same-width edit:

```text
horizontalGridLines, labels-label-leaders, points, labels
```

At pixel ratio 1, **167 of 120,000 pixels change** in the synthetic reproduction. The deliberately thick, distinct strokes make the overlap visible. No chart data, dimensions, label options, or grid options changed.

The existing Gapminder country-label example exhibits the same child-order change; its particular geometry has zero changed pixels at ratio 1, so it alone is not sufficient as a visual regression fixture.

## Expected behavior

Grid/leader layering should be consistent for initial authoring, rematerialization, and equivalent action order. An unchanged canvas width must not change which stroke covers the other.

## Cause and regression coverage

[Label leader materialization](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/actions/marks/text/layout.js#L170) removes and recreates the leader graphic before the source mark. [Grid placement](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/actions/guides/grids/resolve.js) independently chooses a mark anchor without accounting for the generated leaders already preceding it.

Use a shared deterministic ordering policy or preserve valid placement across leader refresh. Test both creation orders, same-width edits, scale edits, actual crossing strokes, child-order equality, and decoded-pixel equality.

Filed during a repository audit at the maintainer's request. No source fix is included.

## Summary

A facet that successfully creates an inferred shared continuous legend cannot subsequently edit its guide policy or scale resolution. Even disabling the shared legend fails with a child-canvas margin error.

The parent has enough space for the promoted legend. The failure comes from trying to recreate that parent-owned legend inside the smaller child during rederivation.

## Environment

Source `fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6`, package version 0.0.12, Node.js 22.23.1 on macOS arm64.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";
import { renderToSVG } from "ggaction/svg";

const faceted = chart()
  .createCanvas({
    width: 280, height: 320,
    margin: { top: 36, right: 18, bottom: 50, left: 58 }
  })
  .createData({ values: [
    { g: "A", x: 1, y: 1, c: 10 },
    { g: "A", x: 2, y: 2, c: 20 },
    { g: "B", x: 3, y: 3, c: 30 },
    { g: "B", x: 4, y: 4, c: 40 }
  ] })
  .createScatterPlot({
    id: "points", x: "x", y: "y",
    color: { field: "c", fieldType: "quantitative" },
    guides: false
  })
  .facet({
    field: "g", columns: 2, gap: 20,
    guides: { axes: "outer", legend: "shared" }
  });

renderToSVG(faceted); // succeeds
for (const edit of [
  p => p.editFacetGuides({ axes: "outer", legend: "shared" }),
  p => p.editFacetGuides({ legend: false }),
  p => p.editFacetScales({ x: "independent" })
]) {
  try { edit(faceted); }
  catch (error) { console.log(error.message); }
}
```

All three edits print:

```text
Gradient legend layout requires more Canvas margin space.
```

## Expected behavior

The unchanged guide policy, disabling the legend, and changing x scale resolution should all work on this valid facet. A shared legend belongs to the parent composition; its placement must not require extra right-margin space in every child.

The repository's existing Gapminder `outer-guides` facet reproduces the same failure.

## Cause

- [copyLegendConfig](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/materialization/facetGuides/placement.js#L16) copies the promoted legend configuration onto the parent.
- [facetUnitTemplate](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/actions/facets/actions.js#L58) combines the parent's materialization configs with the first child's graphic/canvas state when rederiving.
- The parent-only shared legend config consequently participates in ordinary child rematerialization, which tries to lay it out in the child's 18-pixel right margin. It fails before the requested new guide policy is composed.

## Suggested regression coverage

Start with a valid inferred shared legend, then edit guide and scale policies repeatedly, including removing the legend. Cover continuous and categorical legend families, outer/every-panel axes, explicit versus inferred legend sources, preserved child dimensions, and earlier-program immutability.

Filed during a repository audit at the maintainer's request. No source fix is included.

## Summary

UTC year/quarter/month bucketing can return `NaN` for a valid finite input timestamp near JavaScript Date's lower representable boundary. The public action succeeds and stores that value in the derived dataset, violating its finite-timestamp output contract.

This is a numeric-boundary case; ordinary-date probes are unaffected.

## Environment

Source `fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6`, package version 0.0.12, Node.js 22.23.1 on macOS arm64.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const timestamp = -8640000000000000;
console.log(new Date(timestamp).toISOString()); // valid Date

for (const unit of ["year", "quarter", "month", "day"]) {
  const p = chart()
    .createData({ values: [{ timestamp }] })
    .createTimeUnitData({
      id: "bucketed", field: "timestamp", unit, as: "bucket"
    });
  const bucket = p.semanticSpec.datasets.at(-1).values[0].bucket;
  console.log(unit, bucket, Number.isFinite(bucket));
}
```

Actual: year, quarter, and month produce `NaN, false`; day produces a finite timestamp. The same three failures reproduce one millisecond and one day after the lower boundary.

## Expected behavior

The [current contract](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/agent_docs/contract/current/CORE.md#createtimeunitdata) promises a finite UTC bucket timestamp. If the beginning of the requested calendar bucket is outside the representable Date range, reject the operation with a clear range error before storing an invalid derived dataset.

Do not serialize an invalid numeric result silently: `JSON.stringify` turns the stored `NaN` into `null`, which also obscures the failure for downstream consumers.

## Cause and regression coverage

[floorUtcTimeUnit](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/grammar/timeUnit.js#L72) validates the input Date, but returns `utcTimestamp(...)` without validating the result. Flooring year/quarter/month can move a valid input outside the representable interval.

Check both representable date limits and adjacent values for all seven units, finite successful results, clear rejection for unrepresentable bucket starts, source immutability, and downstream temporal encoding.

Filed during a repository audit at the maintainer's request. No source fix is included.

## Summary

The documented ID grammar accepts ordinary JavaScript Object-prototype names, but a fresh chart rejects these mark/graphic IDs as already existing. Dataset, scale, and coordinate creation accept the same IDs, making resource behavior inconsistent.

## Environment

Source `fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6`, package version 0.0.12, Node.js 22.23.1 on macOS arm64.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

for (const id of ["normal", "constructor", "toString", "hasOwnProperty", "valueOf", "__proto__"]) {
  const base = chart()
    .createCanvas({ width: 300, height: 200 })
    .createData({ values: [{ x: 1, y: 2 }] });
  try {
    base.createPointMark({ id });
    console.log(id, "accepted");
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(id, error.message);
  }
}
```

Actual output: `normal` succeeds; every other entry throws `Graphic "<id>" already exists.` No such own graphic was created.

## Expected behavior

The same documented user ID grammar should apply consistently. A resource existence check should test own stored resources, not inherited JavaScript properties. These names contain only the permitted letters and underscores; there is no documented reserved-name exclusion.

## Cause

- [validateUserId](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/core/identifiers.js) accepts `[A-Za-z0-9_-]+`.
- [assertMarkAvailable](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/actions/marks/shared.js#L62) uses `program.graphicSpec.objects[id] !== undefined`.
- [createGraphics](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/actions/primitives/createGraphics.js) uses the same prototype-sensitive lookup.
- The graphics dictionary has an ordinary Object prototype, so names such as `constructor` resolve to inherited members.

This report concerns false resource collisions; it does not claim a prototype-pollution exploit.

## Suggested regression coverage

Check valid prototype-name IDs through create/edit/remove/recreate, own-resource collision detection, graphics placement, serialization, and retained composition. Preserve deterministic IDs and genuine duplicate rejection. Audit other resource dictionaries for the same own-property assumption.

Filed during a repository audit at the maintainer's request. No source fix is included.

## Summary

Assigning a constant opacity to layer B deletes the opacity legend owned by layer A, even though A's field-driven opacity encoding has not changed.

## Environment

Source `fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6`, package version 0.0.12, Node.js 22.23.1 on macOS arm64.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const before = chart()
  .createCanvas({
    width: 760, height: 460,
    margin: { top: 100, bottom: 100, left: 150, right: 150 }
  })
  .createData({
    values: [{ x: 0, y: 0, c: 0 }, { x: 50, y: 50, c: 50 }]
  })
  .createScatterPlot({ id: "a", x: "x", y: "y", guides: false })
  .encodeOpacity({ target: "a", field: "c" })
  .createLegend({ target: "a", channels: ["opacity"] })
  .createScatterPlot({
    id: "b", data: "data", x: "x", y: "y", guides: false
  });

console.log(before.guideConfigs.legend?.opacity?.target); // "a"
const after = before.encodeOpacity({ target: "b", value: 0.5 });
console.log(after.guideConfigs.legend?.opacity); // undefined
console.log(after.semanticSpec.layers.find(l => l.id === "a").encoding.opacity);
// The original field-driven encoding is still present.
```

## Expected behavior

Only B's opacity should change. A's legend config and graphics should remain. If a constant replaces the opacity encoding of the actual legend owner, that owner's matching legend should be removed.

## Actual behavior and cause

The constant branch of [encodeOpacity](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/actions/encodings/appearance.js#L196) checks only whether any opacity legend exists:

```js
const withoutLegend = this.guideConfigs.legend?.opacity === undefined
  ? this
  : this.removeOpacityLegend();
```

It never checks the legend's `target`. The action consequently removes the unrelated block and its graphics. The original immutable program remains intact; the returned program is wrong.

## Suggested regression coverage

- Constant opacity on an unrelated point while another point owns the opacity legend.
- Constant opacity on a supported rule while a point owns that legend.
- Constant replacement on the actual owner.
- Coexisting legend blocks and preserved layout/style.

Filed during a repository audit at the maintainer's request. No source fix is included.

## Summary

`removeMark`, `removeEncoding`, and assigning an encoding to a new scale do not recompute the automatic domain of the scale that lost a consumer. Remaining marks continue to use values belonging to a removed or detached layer.

This differs from #64: here the old **resolved scale itself remains stale**, in addition to its graphics. #64 concerns refreshing sibling graphics when a scale has already been recomputed.

## Environment

Source `fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6`, package version 0.0.12, Node.js 22.23.1 on macOS arm64.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const before = chart()
  .createCanvas({ width: 400, height: 300, margin: 20 })
  .createData({ id: "low", values: [{ x: 0, y: 0 }, { x: 50, y: 50 }] })
  .createData({ id: "high", values: [{ x: 100, y: 100 }, { x: 200, y: 200 }] })
  .createScatterPlot({
    id: "lowPoints", data: "low",
    x: { field: "x", scale: { nice: false } },
    y: { field: "y", scale: { nice: false } },
    guides: false
  })
  .createScatterPlot({
    id: "highPoints", data: "high", x: "x", y: "y", guides: false
  });

const removed = before.removeMark({ target: "highPoints" });
console.log(removed.resolvedScales.x.domain); // [0, 200] -- stale
console.log(removed.graphicSpec.objects.lowPoints.items[1].properties.x); // 110
const refreshed = removed.editCanvas({ width: 400 });
console.log(refreshed.resolvedScales.x.domain); // [0, 50]
console.log(refreshed.graphicSpec.objects.lowPoints.items[1].properties.x); // 380

// Each of these independently leaves the old x scale stale too:
const unbound = before.removeEncoding({ target: "highPoints", channel: "x" });
const moved = before.encodeX({
  target: "highPoints", field: "x", scale: { id: "independentX" }
});
console.log(unbound.resolvedScales.x.domain, moved.resolvedScales.x.domain);
// [0, 200], [0, 200]
```

## Expected behavior

The old named scale definition should remain, as documented. When it still has consumers and `domain: "auto"`, its resolved domain should reflect their current eligible values. With only the low layer consuming x, that domain is `[0, 50]`; value 50 maps to x=380.

Explicit domains must remain explicit. Unused named scales can be retained without attempting to resolve a domain from zero consumers.

## Additional verified cases

The same stale-domain behavior occurs for both x and y. It also occurs for color, size, and opacity after each of these transitions:

1. remove the high layer;
2. remove its appearance encoding;
3. move that encoding to a new scale ID.

For those appearance probes, low values are `[0, 50]` and high values are `[100, 200]`. The surviving old scale stays at `[0, 200]`; an explicit `rematerializeScale({ id: channel })` recomputes `[0, 50]`. A canvas edit alone does not refresh appearance domains, so it is not a general workaround.

## Cause and regression scope

- [removeMark](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/actions/marks/remove.js) removes owned layers/graphics and cleans up guides, but does not refresh scales losing consumers.
- [removeEncoding](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/actions/encodings/remove.js) rebuilds the target and source-dependent marks, without resolving the surviving old scale.
- Encoding reassignment materializes the new scale, omitting dependency invalidation for the previous scale.

Suggested coverage: all three transitions across position and appearance channels; remaining siblings and retained legends/axes; explicit domains; no remaining consumers; immutable earlier programs. Assert numeric/domain values against a freshly authored equivalent chart, not only against a rematerialization helper.

Filed during a repository audit at the maintainer's request. No source fix is included.

## Summary

Two public action paths update a shared resolved scale without refreshing its existing consumers. The semantic domain and the concrete graphics then disagree, so a chart silently displays incorrect positions, colors, sizes, or opacities.

This is related to #34, but the secondary-position/position-encoding path fixed in 019fb823 is not the trigger here. The remaining paths are layer-data rematerialization and appearance-encoding rematerialization.

## Version and environment

- Source: `fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6` (main; package version 0.0.12)
- Node.js 22.23.1, macOS arm64
- Observed in `graphicSpec`; the filtering case was also exported to PNG and SVG
- Synthetic inline data only

## Minimal reproduction

Run this as an ES module from the repository checkout, or change the import to the package entry in a consumer project:

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

function base() {
  return chart()
    .createCanvas({ width: 400, height: 300, margin: 20 })
    .createData({
      id: "low",
      values: [{ x: 0, y: 0, c: 0 }, { x: 50, y: 50, c: 50 }]
    })
    .createData({
      id: "high",
      values: [{ x: 100, y: 100, c: 100 }, { x: 200, y: 200, c: 200 }]
    })
    .createScatterPlot({
      id: "lowPoints", data: "low",
      x: { field: "x", scale: { nice: false } },
      y: { field: "y", scale: { nice: false } },
      guides: false
    })
    .createScatterPlot({
      id: "highPoints", data: "high",
      x: "x", y: "y", guides: false
    });
}

const filtered = base().filterMarks({
  target: "highPoints", field: "x", op: "eq", value: 100
});
console.log("domains", filtered.resolvedScales.x.domain,
  filtered.resolvedScales.y.domain);
console.log("actual", filtered.graphicSpec.objects.lowPoints.items[1].properties);
console.log("after same-width edit",
  filtered.editCanvas({ width: 400 })
    .graphicSpec.objects.lowPoints.items[1].properties);

for (const [method, property, extra] of [
  ["encodeColor", "fill", { fieldType: "quantitative" }],
  ["encodeSize", "radius", {}],
  ["encodeOpacity", "opacity", {}]
]) {
  const p = base()
    [method]({ target: "lowPoints", field: "c", ...extra })
    [method]({ target: "highPoints", field: "c", ...extra });
  console.log(method,
    p.graphicSpec.objects.lowPoints.items[1].properties[property],
    p.editCanvas({ width: 400 })
      .graphicSpec.objects.lowPoints.items[1].properties[property]);
}
```

## Expected and actual results

Filtering contracts the automatic x/y domains from `[0, 200]` to `[0, 100]`. The existing point at data value `(50, 50)` must move to the midpoint of the plot, `(200, 150)`, but stays at `(110, 215)`.

Adding the second appearance consumer expands the corresponding domain to `[0, 200]`. For the first layer's value `c = 50`:

| Channel | Actual stale value | Expected under the final scale |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Color | `#fde725` | `#3b528b` |
| Size (radius) | `7.898654169668588` | `4.61809077155419` |
| Opacity | `1` | `0.4` |

The expected position/opacity values follow direct linear interpolation. Expected radius is `sqrt((24 + 0.25 * (196 - 24)) / PI)`. Expected color is the first-quarter position in the resolved palette. A same-width `editCanvas({ width: 400 })` corrects all four cases, even though the canvas dimensions and the relevant chart meaning have not changed.

The original program and caller-owned input rows remain unchanged; this is a dependency-refresh bug, not input mutation.

## Cause in the current source

- [planLayerDataRematerialization](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/materialization/dependencies.js#L67) resolves the affected scales with `marks: false`, but schedules only the target mark and source-dependent marks. Other layers sharing those scales are omitted.
- [getEncodingMaterializationStages](https://github.com/ggaction/ggaction/blob/fc45c3d5f518c8acc1ce4555d6db0896e56899c6/src/materialization/marks/index.js#L105) also schedules `marks: false`; sibling enumeration is conditional on mark-policy `sharedChannels`, which does not cover these point encodings.
- The existing position-encoding planner already enumerates complete shared position consumers, explaining why #34's fix does not cover these paths.

## Suggested regression coverage

1. Filter one of two independently sourced layers sharing automatic x/y scales; check both layers against independently calculated final coordinates.
2. Add/reassign color, size, and opacity encodings on a second consumer; check earlier consumers against the final scale.
3. Cover domain contraction as well as expansion, mixed supported mark types, guides, and source-dependent marks.
4. Check both action orders, immutability, and equality with a full rematerialization.
5. Preserve explicit domains and unrelated independent-scale layers.

Baseline verification before these probes: all 2,273 normal tests, 137 rendering tests, 53 browser tests, and 167 realistic tests passed. Existing green suites do not exercise these shared-consumer transitions.

Filed during a repository audit at the maintainer's request. No source fix is included.

## Summary

`createErrorBar` rejects a quantitative x position, which prevents vertical confidence intervals at observations on a numeric or transformed x scale (for example a base-2 learning-curve axis).

## Environment

- ggaction 0.0.11
- Node.js 23.5.0

## Minimal reproduction

Save this as `repro.mjs` in a project with ggaction 0.0.11 and run `node repro.mjs`:

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const rows = [
  { training_charts: 100, estimate: 0.42, lower: 0.38, upper: 0.46 },
  { training_charts: 200, estimate: 0.51, lower: 0.47, upper: 0.55 }
];

chart()
  .createCanvas({ width: 500, height: 320, margin: 50 })
  .createData({ id: "raw", values: rows })
  .createErrorBar({
    data: "raw",
    x: {
      field: "training_charts",
      fieldType: "quantitative",
      scale: { id: "x", zero: false }
    },
    y: {
      center: "estimate",
      lower: "lower",
      upper: "upper",
      scale: { id: "y", zero: false }
    },
    caps: true
  });
```

## Actual behavior

```text
Error: createErrorBar x position requires nominal, ordinal, temporal field type.
```

## Expected behavior

A vertical error bar should accept a quantitative x position, with the rule and both caps sharing the quantitative x scale. This should also remain valid when that scale is subsequently edited to `type: "log"` with `base: 2`.

## Workaround

The same geometry can be authored from ggaction primitives with `createRuleMark`, `encodeY2`, and two horizontal `createTickMark` layers, but the high-level `createErrorBar` action should support this standard use case directly.

## Summary

When a child chart has a categorical legend configured with `position: "bottom"`, promoting it through `facet({ guides: { legend: "shared" } })` always reserves a right-side legend lane. The requested legend position is not preserved, and a one-column narrow facet becomes substantially wider than its panels.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const rows = [
  { panel: "A", category: "one", model: "0.5B", value: 1 },
  { panel: "A", category: "two", model: "1.5B", value: 2 },
  { panel: "B", category: "one", model: "0.5B", value: 2 },
  { panel: "B", category: "two", model: "1.5B", value: 3 }
];

const base = chart()
  .createCanvas({
    width: 300,
    height: 300,
    margin: { top: 30, right: 20, bottom: 100, left: 50 }
  })
  .createData({ id: "raw", values: rows })
  .createPointMark({ id: "points" })
  .encodeX({ field: "category", fieldType: "ordinal" })
  .encodeY({ field: "value" })
  .encodeShape({ field: "model" })
  .createLegend({
    target: "points",
    channels: ["shape"],
    position: "bottom",
    direction: "horizontal"
  });

const faceted = base.facet({
  field: "panel",
  columns: 1,
  guides: { axes: "each", legend: "shared" }
});

console.log(faceted.graphicSpec.objects.canvas.properties.width);
console.log(faceted.graphicSpec.objects["facet-shared-legend"]?.properties);
```

## Expected behavior

Either:

1. preserve the promoted legend's requested `position: "bottom"`, placing it below the facet grid, or
2. expose a facet-level shared-legend position option.

## Actual behavior

The shared legend is always laid out to the right of the facet grid. With `columns: 1`, it sits beside the vertical panel stack and expands the responsive figure width. The current workaround is to disable shared promotion, remove the legend from all but the final child, and retain one bottom legend there.

## Environment

- ggaction: 0.0.11
- Node.js: 23.5.0
- macOS

### Bug

`renderToPDF()` misplaces text in an `hconcat` composition when the child canvases use different margins. SVG renders the same composition correctly.

### Minimal reproduction

```js
import fs from "node:fs";
import { chart, hconcat } from "ggaction";
import { renderToPDF } from "ggaction/pdf";
import { renderToSVG } from "ggaction/svg";

function panel(title, leftMargin) {
  return chart()
    .createCanvas({
      width: 240,
      height: 160,
      margin: { top: 58, right: 16, bottom: 24, left: leftMargin },
      background: "white"
    })
    .createTitle({ text: title, position: "top", align: "left", gap: 0 });
}

const comparison = hconcat({
  id: "comparison",
  programs: [
    { id: "left", program: panel("Left panel", 50) },
    { id: "right", program: panel("Right panel", 10) }
  ],
  gap: 20,
  align: "start",
  padding: 0
});

fs.writeFileSync("repro.svg", renderToSVG(comparison));
await renderToPDF(comparison, { output: "repro.pdf" });
```

### Expected

The PDF should match the SVG: `Left panel` starts at the first canvas's 50 px left margin, and `Right panel` starts at the second canvas's 10 px left margin after the horizontal composition offset.

### Actual

The SVG is correct, but the PDF places the titles at the same/incorrect horizontal origin; only the overlaid `Right panel` title is visible near the left side. The same problem affects axis labels and chart titles in a fuller two-panel chart.

Using identical margins for both children can mask the issue. A temporary workaround is to export SVG/PNG first and convert that output to PDF.

### Environment

- ggaction: 0.0.10
- Node.js: 23.5.0
- macOS (Apple Silicon)
